course: language
lesson: 22
title: Where English came from
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: English was not always in England: about 1,500 years ago Angles, Saxons and Jutes sailed over the North Sea from what is now Denmark, northern Germany and the Netherlands, and their speech became Old English.
explain: Old English looks like a secret code today; the famous poem "Beowulf" begins "Hwæt! We Gardena in geardagum", and we need a translation to read it.
explain: Languages change a little every generation, like a face in a flip-book; in 1066 French-speaking Normans took over England, thousands of French words poured in, and Old English turned into Middle English.
explain: Later the vowel sounds slid about, the printing press arrived, and spelling got stuck while the sounds kept moving, which is one reason English spelling is so odd today.
explain: Your great-great-great-grandparents' English already sounded a bit different from yours, and your grandchildren's English will sound a bit different again.
fact: In the 400s and 500s AD, Angles, Saxons and Jutes settled in Britain; their language became Old English, spoken from about 450 to 1100.
fact: The Vikings came later, from around 800 AD, and they were a different people from the Anglo-Saxons.
fact: "Beowulf" is an Old English poem about a hero, written down about 1,000 years ago; today it has to be translated.
fact: English is a Germanic language, a cousin of German, Dutch and Frisian: house is "Haus" in German and "huis" in Dutch, and water is "Wasser" and "water".
fact: In 1066 the Normans from France conquered England; for about 300 years the rulers mostly spoke French, while most people kept speaking English.
fact: The Normans did not ban English; it lived on and took in thousands of French words, becoming Middle English, spoken from about 1100 to 1500.
fact: Chaucer wrote "The Canterbury Tales" in Middle English in the 1300s; "Whan that Aprill with his shoures soote" means "When April with its sweet showers".
fact: Words change meaning: "meat" once meant any food, which is why old books say "meat and drink" and a "sweetmeat" is a sweet.
fact: Between about 1400 and 1700 English vowel sounds changed a lot (the Great Vowel Shift): "bite" was once said a bit like "beet" and "house" a bit like "hoose".
fact: William Caxton brought the printing press to England in 1476; printers had to choose spellings, and many of their choices stuck.
fact: In "Ye Olde Tea Shoppe" the Y stands for an old letter called thorn (þ), which made a "th" sound, so people always said "the".
fact: Shakespeare's plays are the earliest written record found so far for some words, like "bedroom" and "eyeball"; many may already have been spoken, so "first recorded" does not always mean "invented".
fact: Mandarin Chinese has the most native speakers (people who learned it from birth); English has the most speakers in total if you count people who learned it later, about 1.5 billion by many estimates.
exercise: About how long ago did the people whose speech became English sail to Britain? | About 1,500 years ago | Only about 150 years ago | Roughly 5,000 years ago | Only about 500 years ago
exercise: Liam asks if the Anglo-Saxons were Vikings. What's the answer? | No, the Vikings were a different people who came later | Yes, they were the very same people, just with two different names | Yes, but only the ones who sailed over from Spain | No, the Vikings came first, hundreds of years before the Anglo-Saxons
exercise: Ivy reads "Hwæt! We Gardena in geardagum" and says it's not English. What is it? | Old English, the start of "Beowulf" | Old German from a drinking song | Viking runes carved on a stone | Latin from a prayer book in a church
exercise: Why does "house" sound like German "Haus"? | English and German are related Germanic languages | English copied lots of German words only last century | Germans copied English words from Hollywood films | It is pure coincidence, like two strangers looking alike
exercise: What happened to English after the Normans conquered England in 1066? | It took in thousands of French words | It was banned by law and disappeared for good | It turned into French completely within ten years | Nothing changed at all
exercise: For about how long did England's rulers mostly speak French after 1066? | About 300 years | Only about 3 years | Only about 30 years | About 3,000 years
exercise: What kind of English did Chaucer write in the 1300s? | Middle English | Old English | Modern English | Viking English
exercise: In Chaucer's line, what does "soote" mean? | Sweet | Soot | Sour | Soon
exercise: Why do old books say "meat and drink"? | "Meat" once meant any food | People only ate meat back then | It was a spelling mistake | "Drink" once meant meat
exercise: What was the Great Vowel Shift? | A big change in English vowel sounds from about 1400 to 1700 | A law made by an English king that removed several vowels from spelling | A move of the letters of the alphabet into a brand-new order by printers | A change from Latin to English letters
exercise: Who brought the printing press to England in 1476? | William Caxton | William the Conqueror | William Shakespeare | Geoffrey Chaucer
exercise: How should you read "Ye Olde Tea Shoppe"? | "The old tea shop" | "Yee old-ee tea shop-ee" | "Thee old tea shop" | "Yes, old tea shop"
exercise: What was the old letter þ called? | Thorn | Thistle | Thunder | Thursday
exercise: Shakespeare is the first known written record for "eyeball". What does that tell us for sure? | It is the earliest place found so far where it was written | He definitely invented the word himself, so nobody had ever said it | Nobody in England said "eyeball" before he was born in 1564 | The word was banned before him
exercise: Which language has the most native speakers? | Mandarin Chinese | English | Spanish | French
exercise: What is a native speaker? | Someone who learned the language from birth | Someone who speaks the language very loudly and clearly | Someone who learned the language at school as a teenager | Someone who only reads it
test: Your mate goes "English has always been in England, it's English, innit." What's true? | It arrived over the sea with Germanic settlers about 1,500 years ago | He's right, it has been spoken in England since the very first people | It came from Latin-speaking Romans, who brought it about 2,000 years ago | It came with the Vikings first
test: Ollie reckons "the Normans banned English, that's why we talk French words". What's true? | English survived and borrowed many French words | He's right, English was banned for about 300 years by law | English was banned, but came back secretly in 1100 | The Normans spoke English already
test: What would happen if you went back 1,000 years and chatted to an English farmer? | You would struggle to understand each other | You would understand every single word he said | You would both speak modern English with no trouble | He would only use sign language
test: Rosa's bro ("bro" means brother) says Middle English is "impossible, it's a different language". What's fairer? | With patience and some help you can guess a lot of it | He's right, nobody can read it | It's actually just Latin | It's just modern English with typos
test: Grandad says "back in my day words meant what they meant, forever." What's true? | Words change meaning over time, like "meat" once meaning any food | He's right, words never change their meaning once they are in a dictionary | Only slang words change, and proper dictionary words always stay the same | Words change only when a king orders it
test: In a game, a knight in 1300 says "bite" exactly like we do. What's off? | In 1300 "bite" sounded a bit like "beet" | Knights could not speak, they only wrote | Nothing, the sounds of words never change over time | Knights only spoke Latin
test: A tea shop sign says "Ye Olde Sweete Shoppe" and a lad laughs "they said YE back then lol". What's true? | They said "the"; the Y stood for the old letter thorn | He's right, people really said "ye" when they meant "the" | The Y was just a decoration that sign painters liked to add | "Ye" meant "yes" in old times
test: Your cousin from Yorkshire says "Shakespeare made up every word, he's mint." ("Mint" means great.) What's fairer? | He was very creative, but some of his "first" words were probably already spoken | Yes, he invented all English words | No, he never wrote any new words | Yes, and he invented the alphabet too
test: Dad says English is "the biggest language on Earth, end of." Which is true? | It depends: Mandarin has most native speakers, English most speakers overall | Dad is right: English has the most native speakers and the most speakers overall | Spanish is the biggest by every measure, native speakers and learners alike | French has the most speakers overall
test: Why might English spelling be so weird today? | Spelling got stuck by printers while the sounds kept changing | Printers chose new spellings at random every single day, just for fun | English never had any rules at all, so everyone spelled however they wanted | Children changed the spellings for fun
test: What would English be like if the Normans had never come? | It would probably have far fewer French words | It would be exactly the same as it is today, word for word | It would be French | It would have no words at all for food, law or kings
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origins of the English language? | It derives from the speech of Germanic settlers who arrived in Britain in the fifth century | It was the original language of the indigenous Britons since the most remote ages of prehistory | It derives directly and solely from Latin as spoken by the Roman garrisons stationed in Britain | It was introduced by the Vikings in the eleventh century
exam: Identify the principal consequence for the English language of the Norman Conquest of 1066. | The incorporation of a very great number of French words | The total prohibition and extinction of English | The adoption of French as the sole language of the populace | The disappearance of all Germanic vocabulary
exam: In respect of the inscription "Ye Olde Tea Shoppe", select the response consistent with the established facts. | The initial letter represents the obsolete character thorn and the word is pronounced "the" | The word was pronounced "ye" by all mediaeval speakers, precisely as the spelling plainly indicates | The letter Y was a purely decorative addition of no significance, much favoured by sign painters | The word signifies the pronoun "you"
exam: Determine which language possesses the greatest number of native speakers. | Mandarin Chinese | English | Spanish | Hindi
